NNAAMMEE
       umount - unmount filesystems

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       uummoouunntt --aa [--ddffllnnrrvv] [--tt _f_s_t_y_p_e] [--OO _o_p_t_i_o_n...]

       uummoouunntt [--ddffllnnrrvv] {_d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y|_d_e_v_i_c_e}

       uummoouunntt --hh|--VV

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The uummoouunntt command detaches the mentioned filesystem(s) from the file
       hierarchy. A filesystem is specified by giving the directory where it
       has been mounted. Giving the special device on which the filesystem
       lives may also work, but is obsolete, mainly because it will fail in
       case this device was mounted on more than one directory.

       Note that a filesystem cannot be unmounted when it is 'busy' - for
       example, when there are open files on it, or when some process has its
       working directory there, or when a swap file on it is in use. The
       offending process could even be uummoouunntt itself - it opens libc, and libc
       in its turn may open for example locale files. A lazy unmount avoids
       this problem, but it may introduce other issues. See ----llaazzyy description
       below.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --aa, ----aallll
           All of the filesystems described in _/_p_r_o_c_/_s_e_l_f_/_m_o_u_n_t_i_n_f_o (or in
           deprecated _/_e_t_c_/_m_t_a_b) are unmounted, except the proc, devfs,
           devpts, sysfs, rpc_pipefs and nfsd filesystems. This list of the
           filesystems may be replaced by ----ttyyppeess umount option.

       --AA, ----aallll--ttaarrggeettss
           Unmount all mountpoints in the current mount namespace for the
           specified filesystem. The filesystem can be specified by one of the
           mountpoints or the device name (or UUID, etc.). When this option is
           used together with ----rreeccuurrssiivvee, then all nested mounts within the
           filesystem are recursively unmounted. This option is only supported
           on systems where _/_e_t_c_/_m_t_a_b is a symlink to _/_p_r_o_c_/_m_o_u_n_t_s.

       --cc, ----nnoo--ccaannoonniiccaalliizzee
           Do not canonicalize paths. The paths canonicalization is based on
           ssttaatt(2) and rreeaaddlliinnkk(2) system calls. These system calls may hang
           in some cases (for example on NFS if server is not available). The
           option has to be used with canonical path to the mount point.

           This option is silently ignored by uummoouunntt for non-root users.

           For more details about this option see the mmoouunntt(8) man page. Note
           that uummoouunntt does not pass this option to the //ssbbiinn//uummoouunntt.._t_y_p_e
           helpers.

       --dd, ----ddeettaacchh--lloooopp
           When the unmounted device was a loop device, also free this loop
           device. This option is unnecessary for devices initialized by
           mmoouunntt(8), in this case "autoclear" functionality is enabled by
           default.

       ----ffaakkee
           Causes everything to be done except for the actual system call or
           umount helper execution; this 'fakes' unmounting the filesystem. It
           can be used to remove entries from the deprecated _/_e_t_c_/_m_t_a_b that
           were unmounted earlier with the --nn option.

       --ff, ----ffoorrccee
           Force an unmount (in case of an unreachable NFS system).

           Note that this option does not guarantee that umount command does
           not hang. It’s strongly recommended to use absolute paths without
           symlinks to avoid unwanted rreeaaddlliinnkk(2) and ssttaatt(2) system calls on
           unreachable NFS in uummoouunntt.

       --ii, ----iinntteerrnnaall--oonnllyy
           Do not call the //ssbbiinn//uummoouunntt.._f_i_l_e_s_y_s_t_e_m helper even if it exists.
           By default such a helper program is called if it exists.

       --ll, ----llaazzyy
           Lazy unmount. Detach the filesystem from the file hierarchy now,
           and clean up all references to this filesystem as soon as it is not
           busy anymore.

           A system reboot would be expected in near future if you’re going to
           use this option for network filesystem or local filesystem with
           submounts. The recommended use-case for uummoouunntt --ll is to prevent
           hangs on shutdown due to an unreachable network share where a
           normal uummoouunntt will hang due to a downed server or a network
           partition. Remounts of the share will not be possible.

       --NN, ----nnaammeessppaaccee _n_s
           Perform uummoouunntt in the mount namespace specified by _n_s. _n_s is either
           PID of process running in that namespace or special file
           representing that namespace.

           uummoouunntt switches to the namespace when it reads _/_e_t_c_/_f_s_t_a_b, writes
           _/_e_t_c_/_m_t_a_b (or writes to _/_r_u_n_/_m_o_u_n_t) and calls uummoouunntt(2) system
           call, otherwise it runs in the original namespace. It means that
           the target mount namespace does not have to contain any libraries
           or other requirements necessary to execute uummoouunntt(2) command.

           See mmoouunntt__nnaammeessppaacceess(7) for more information.

       --nn, ----nnoo--mmttaabb
           Unmount without writing in _/_e_t_c_/_m_t_a_b.

       --OO, ----tteesstt--ooppttss _o_p_t_i_o_n...
           Unmount only the filesystems that have the specified option set in
           _/_e_t_c_/_f_s_t_a_b. More than one option may be specified in a
           comma-separated list. Each option can be prefixed with nnoo to
           indicate that no action should be taken for this option.

       --qq, ----qquuiieett
           Suppress "not mounted" error messages.

       --RR, ----rreeccuurrssiivvee
           Recursively unmount each specified directory. Recursion for each
           directory will stop if any unmount operation in the chain fails for
           any reason. The relationship between mountpoints is determined by
           _/_p_r_o_c_/_s_e_l_f_/_m_o_u_n_t_i_n_f_o entries. The filesystem must be specified by
           mountpoint path; a recursive unmount by device name (or UUID) is
           unsupported. Since version 2.37 it umounts also all over-mounted
           filesystems (more filesystems on the same mountpoint).

       --rr, ----rreeaadd--oonnllyy
           When an unmount fails, try to remount the filesystem read-only.

       --tt, ----ttyyppeess _t_y_p_e...
           Indicate that the actions should only be taken on filesystems of
           the specified _t_y_p_e. More than one type may be specified in a
           comma-separated list. The list of filesystem types can be prefixed
           with nnoo to indicate that no action should be taken for all of the
           mentioned types. Note that uummoouunntt reads information about mounted
           filesystems from kernel (_/_p_r_o_c_/_m_o_u_n_t_s) and filesystem names may be
           different than filesystem names used in the _/_e_t_c_/_f_s_t_a_b (e.g.,
           "nfs4" vs. "nfs").

       --vv, ----vveerrbboossee
           Verbose mode.

       --hh, ----hheellpp
           Display help text and exit.

       --VV, ----vveerrssiioonn
           Print version and exit.

NNOONN--SSUUPPEERRUUSSEERR UUMMOOUUNNTTSS
       Normally, only the superuser can umount filesystems. However, when
       _f_s_t_a_b contains the uusseerr option on a line, anybody can umount the
       corresponding filesystem. For more details see mmoouunntt(8) man page.

       Since version 2.34 the uummoouunntt command can be used to perform umount
       operation also for fuse filesystems if kernel mount table contains
       user’s ID. In this case _f_s_t_a_b uusseerr== mount option is not required.

       Since version 2.35 uummoouunntt command does not exit when user permissions
       are inadequate by internal lliibbmmoouunntt security rules. It drops suid
       permissions and continue as regular non-root user. This can be used to
       support use-cases where root permissions are not necessary (e.g., fuse
       filesystems, user namespaces, etc).

LLOOOOPP DDEEVVIICCEE
       The uummoouunntt command will automatically detach loop device previously
       initialized by mmoouunntt(8) command independently of _/_e_t_c_/_m_t_a_b.

       In this case the device is initialized with "autoclear" flag (see
       lloosseettuupp(8) output for more details), otherwise it’s necessary to use
       the option ----ddeettaacchh--lloooopp or call lloosseettuupp --dd _d_e_v_i_c_e. The autoclear
       feature is supported since Linux 2.6.25.

